screen.METEORS=Meteors
screen.METEOR_SHOWER=Perseid Shower
screen.METEOR_SHAPE=Meteor Shape
option.ENABLE_STARS=Procedural Stars
option.ENABLE_STARS.comment=Procedural starfield, needs no textures. Turn off to keep only the real star map's galaxy and bright stars.
option.STARS_INTENSITY=Star Brightness
option.STARS_INTENSITY.comment=Peak brightness of a star. Too high and every star exceeds the bloom threshold and turns into a glowing ball.
option.STARS_SCALE=Star Density
option.STARS_SCALE.comment=Cube-cell edge length: higher means more, smaller stars. Too high and the stars become small enough for TAA to erase.
option.STARS_MIN_PIXEL=Min Star Size
option.STARS_MIN_PIXEL.comment=A star must cover at least this many pixels. Below ~2 the isolated dot gets clamped away by TAA and the sky looks starless.
option.STARS_COVERAGE=Cell Coverage
option.STARS_COVERAGE.comment=Chance that a given cell contains a star.
option.STARS_BRIGHT_RATIO=Bright Star Ratio
option.STARS_BRIGHT_RATIO.comment=Among existing stars, the fraction promoted to clearly bright ones. Creates the "many faint + a few bright" layering.
option.STARS_TWINKLE=Twinkle Amount
option.STARS_TWINKLE.comment=Strength of star twinkling. Too high and the night sky looks like noise.
option.STARS_FADE_SUNRISE=Stars Start Fading (sun height)
option.STARS_FADE_SUNRISE.comment=Above this sun elevation stars begin to fade. Negative means they start fading while the sun is still below the horizon.
option.STARS_FADE_SUNSET=Stars Fully Gone (sun height)
option.STARS_FADE_SUNSET.comment=Above this sun elevation stars are completely invisible.
option.STARS_HORIZON_FADE=Horizon Extinction
option.STARS_HORIZON_FADE.comment=Near the horizon starlight crosses more air and is scattered away (atmospheric extinction). At 0 the galaxy is cut off with a hard edge at the horizon.
option.ENABLE_GALAXY=Real Galaxy / Bright Stars
option.ENABLE_GALAXY.comment=Uses NASA's Deep Star Maps 2020: real Milky Way structure and real bright star positions. Turn off to keep only the procedural stars.
option.GALAXY_INTENSITY=Galaxy Brightness
option.GALAXY_INTENSITY.comment=Overall star map brightness. The ceiling is about 0.86; above that the brightest stars exceed the bloom threshold and the sky turns into lanterns.
option.GALAXY_LATITUDE=Observer Latitude
option.GALAXY_LATITUDE.comment=Sets the celestial pole height and how the galaxy runs across the sky. 43 deg is a typical northern mid-latitude.
option.GALAXY_SOLAR_POS=Season Phase
option.GALAXY_SOLAR_POS.comment=Which part of the sky is overhead at midnight. 0=spring equinox, 0.25=summer solstice, 0.5=autumn equinox, 0.75=winter solstice.
option.GALAXY_RA_SIGN=Right Ascension Sign
option.GALAXY_RA_SIGN.comment=Set to -1 if the constellations come out mirrored (e.g. the Big Dipper opening the wrong way).
value.GALAXY_RA_SIGN.-1.0=Mirrored
value.GALAXY_RA_SIGN.1.0=Normal
option.GALAXY_DEBUG=Galaxy Debug
option.GALAXY_DEBUG.comment=Shows the raw star map. If you see a pattern the texture loaded (so it is only a brightness issue); all black means it did not load.
option.ENABLE_MOON_PHASE=Moon Phase
option.ENABLE_MOON_PHASE.comment=The moon shows real phases from the world day (0=full, 4=new) and drives both ground moonlight and how much the stars are washed out.
option.MOON_LIMB_SOFTEN=Lunar Limb Softening
option.MOON_LIMB_SOFTEN.comment=Lunar regolith backscatters strongly, so the full moon is nearly uniform rather than darkening to black at the limb like an ideal Lambert sphere. 1.0 = ideal Lambert.
option.MOON_TERMINATOR_SOFT=Terminator Softening
option.MOON_TERMINATOR_SOFT.comment=Width of the light/dark boundary on a crescent. At 0 it is about one pixel wide and visibly aliased.
option.MOON_STAR_OCCLUSION=Full-Moon Star Washout
option.MOON_STAR_OCCLUSION.comment=Light pollution from the moon: stars get sparser on a full-moon night. 0.85 = up to 85% of their brightness removed.
option.MOON_OCCLUDE_GALAXY=Dim Galaxy Too
option.MOON_OCCLUDE_GALAXY.comment=Dim the galaxy along with the stars, otherwise you get "the stars are gone but the galaxy is still there".
option.MOON_LIGHT_MIN=New-Moon Moonlight Floor
option.MOON_LIGHT_MIN.comment=Ground moonlight on a new-moon night, relative to full moon. 0.03 is most realistic but very dark; 0.3+ is easier to navigate by.
option.ENABLE_MOON_ORBIT=Independent Moon Orbit
option.ENABLE_MOON_ORBIT.comment=Off by default. When on, the moon rises and sets on a real orbit (first quarter rises at noon) instead of always sitting opposite the sun. With it off, only the phase changes and the moon stays put.
option.MOON_PHASE_AMOUNT=Orbit Decoupling
option.MOON_PHASE_AMOUNT.comment=How far the moon moves away from the anti-solar point. 1.0 = true elongation, 0 = locked opposite the sun.
option.MOON_INCLINATION=Lunar Orbit Inclination
option.MOON_INCLINATION.comment=Tilt of the moon's orbit to the ecliptic (really 5.145 deg). At 0 every full moon would fall into Earth's shadow.
option.MOON_NODAL_PERIOD=Nodal Period (game days)
option.MOON_NODAL_PERIOD.comment=How fast the inclination direction cycles. The real value is 6798 days, far too slow for Minecraft.
option.ENABLE_MOON_SHADOW=Shadows Follow the Real Moon
option.ENABLE_MOON_SHADOW.comment=Projects the shadow map along the real moon direction, fixing "the shadow is on the right and so is the moon". Daytime is untouched. Only meaningful with the independent orbit enabled.
option.ENABLE_METEORS=Meteors
option.ENABLE_METEORS.comment=Sporadic meteors and showers. The glow comes from the existing bloom pass; no extra post-processing is added.
option.METEOR_RATE=Meteor Rate
option.METEOR_RATE.comment=Meteors per hour outside a shower. The real background rate is about 5-10/hour, but that is one every few minutes. Multiplied by the shower boost at peak.
option.METEOR_BRIGHTNESS=Overall Brightness
option.METEOR_BRIGHTNESS.comment=Scales the whole meteor. Lower makes it less conspicuous.
option.METEOR_YEAR_PHASE=Year Phase (simulated season)
option.METEOR_YEAR_PHASE.comment=Note: GLSL cannot read the real calendar, so this is a SIMULATED season slider, not an ephemeris. 0 = Jan 1; 0.62 is roughly mid August (the Perseid peak window).
option.METEOR_SHOWER_CENTER=Shower Peak Phase
option.METEOR_SHOWER_CENTER.comment=Centre of the peak window in year phase. The default 0.62 corresponds to mid August.
option.METEOR_SHOWER_WIDTH=Shower Window Width
option.METEOR_SHOWER_WIDTH.comment=Half-width of the peak window. 0.02 is about seven days either side.
option.METEOR_SHOWER_BOOST=Peak Boost
option.METEOR_SHOWER_BOOST.comment=How much the rate is multiplied at peak. The Perseids have a ZHR near 100, roughly 10-20x the background.
option.METEOR_RADIANT_RA=Radiant Right Ascension
option.METEOR_RADIANT_RA.comment=Right ascension of the shower radiant in degrees. Perseus is 48. To use another shower change this and the declination.
option.METEOR_RADIANT_DEC=Radiant Declination
option.METEOR_RADIANT_DEC.comment=Declination of the shower radiant in degrees. Perseus is +58.
option.METEOR_SHOWER_MIN_ANGLE=Shower Min Start Angle
option.METEOR_SHOWER_MIN_ANGLE.comment=Closest a shower member starts to the radiant. Too close and they bunch up.
option.METEOR_SHOWER_MAX_ANGLE=Shower Max Start Angle
option.METEOR_SHOWER_MAX_ANGLE.comment=Farthest a shower member starts from the radiant.
option.METEOR_PATH_LEN=Trail Length
option.METEOR_PATH_LEN.comment=Total angle a meteor crosses.
option.METEOR_LIFE=Lifetime
option.METEOR_LIFE.comment=Seconds from appearing to vanishing. Longer means slower, but past about one second it drifts rather than streaks.
option.METEOR_FADE_IN=Fade-In Fraction
option.METEOR_FADE_IN.comment=Portion of the lifetime spent fading in. Keep it small: a meteor should flare on, not glow up like a lamp.
option.METEOR_FADE_START=Fade-Out Start
option.METEOR_FADE_START.comment=Where the fade-out begins. Too large (e.g. 0.8) leaves only a few frames and reads as a hard disappearance.
option.METEOR_TAIL_LEN=Tail Length
option.METEOR_TAIL_LEN.comment=Angular length of the tail. Too short and the meteor degrades into a stubby dash.
option.METEOR_TRAIL_GAIN=Tail Brightness Gain
option.METEOR_TRAIL_GAIN.comment=How bright the line is, and whether it glows. Below about 1.0 even the brightest meteor stays under the bloom threshold and looks more subtle.
option.METEOR_TAIL_FALLOFF=Tail Falloff
option.METEOR_TAIL_FALLOFF.comment=Higher makes the tail shorter and snappier. Too low and the tail drags out long and flat.
option.METEOR_TAIL_END_FADE=Tail End Fade
option.METEOR_TAIL_END_FADE.comment=How early the tail starts fading to zero. Near 1 it leaves a hard edge at the tip.
option.METEOR_WIDTH=Tail Width
option.METEOR_WIDTH.comment=Cross-section of the tail. Watch the lower limit: once it goes sub-pixel TAA treats the whole line as an isolated dot and erases it.
option.METEOR_HEAD_SIZE=Head Size
option.METEOR_HEAD_SIZE.comment=Size of the head glow. At the same scale as the tail it reads as a line; enlarge it and it becomes a glowing ball.
option.METEOR_HEAD_GAIN=Head Brightness Gain
option.METEOR_HEAD_GAIN.comment=Head brightness relative to the tail. At 0 there is no head at all, just a line.
screen.COMETS=Comets
screen.COMET_TAILS=Comet Tails
option.ENABLE_COMETS=Comets
option.ENABLE_COMETS.comment=A real comet is one nucleus plus TWO tails: a blue ion tail pointing straight away from the sun, and a yellow-white dust tail curving back along the orbit. A single straight tail just looks like a meteor.
option.COMET_DEBUG_FORCE=Force Always On (debug)
option.COMET_DEBUG_FORCE.comment=Keeps the comet in the sky permanently, at full brightness and in a fixed position, so its geometry can be tuned. Turn it off before releasing.
option.COMET_PERIOD=Appearance Period (s)
option.COMET_PERIOD.comment=How often the dice are re-rolled for whether a comet appears. Longer means rarer.
option.COMET_CHANCE=Appearance Chance
option.COMET_CHANCE.comment=Probability of a comet in any given period. 0.35 with a 300 s period works out to roughly one every 14 minutes.
option.COMET_WINDOW=Visible Window
option.COMET_WINDOW.comment=Fraction of the period the comet is visible, fading in and out at both ends.
option.COMET_BRIGHTNESS=Overall Brightness
option.COMET_BRIGHTNESS.comment=Scales the whole comet; the view-ray atmospheric transmittance is applied on top.
option.COMET_ELEV_BASE=Comet Elevation Base
option.COMET_ELEV_BASE.comment=Roughly how high (in radians) the comet sits in the sky. Too low and terrain and fog hide it.
option.COMET_HEAD_SIZE=Nucleus Size
option.COMET_HEAD_SIZE.comment=Angular radius of the coma (the glow around the nucleus).
option.COMET_HEAD_GAIN=Nucleus Glow Gain
option.COMET_HEAD_GAIN.comment=Brightness gain of the nucleus. It must exceed the bloom threshold to form that soft halo.
option.COMET_ION_LEN=Ion Tail Length
option.COMET_ION_LEN.comment=Length of the blue ion tail. Real ion tails can stretch very long and straight.
option.COMET_ION_WIDTH=Ion Tail Width
option.COMET_ION_WIDTH.comment=Cross-section of the ion tail, usually narrower than the dust tail.
option.COMET_ION_INTENSITY=Ion Tail Brightness
option.COMET_ION_INTENSITY.comment=Strength of the blue ion tail. On a real comet the ion tail is often just a faint streak, much dimmer than the dust tail.
option.COMET_ION_WIDEST=Ion Tail Widest Half-Width
option.COMET_ION_WIDEST.comment=Sigma (Gaussian half-width) at the fattest point of the ion tail. The solar wind pulls the plasma into a thin filament, so it is clearly narrower than the dust tail.
option.COMET_ION_CURVE=Ion Tail Curvature
option.COMET_ION_CURVE.comment=The solar wind blows the ion tail directly anti-sunward, so it is strictly straight. Default 0.
option.COMET_DUST_LEN=Dust Tail Length
option.COMET_DUST_LEN.comment=Length of the yellow-white dust tail.
option.COMET_DUST_WIDTH=Dust Tail Width
option.COMET_DUST_WIDTH.comment=Cross-section of the dust tail, usually broader than the ion tail.
option.COMET_DUST_INTENSITY=Dust Tail Brightness
option.COMET_DUST_INTENSITY.comment=Strength of the yellow-white dust tail. On a real comet this is usually THE tail you can actually see: radiation pressure spreads the dust into a broad, curved fan while the ion tail stays faint.
option.COMET_DUST_WIDEST=Dust Tail Widest Half-Width
option.COMET_DUST_WIDEST.comment=Sigma (Gaussian half-width) at the fattest point of the dust tail, i.e. the tail's maximum thickness. Measured on a photo: about 0.021 of the tail length.
option.COMET_DUST_CURVE=Dust Tail Curvature
option.COMET_DUST_CURVE.comment=Total bend of the dust tail axis. Measured on a real photo, the dust tail is essentially STRAIGHT (its centreline departs from a straight line by only 0.3-0.5% of the tail length), so the default is 0.01. The bowed look in some photos is a projection effect seen closer to face-on; raise this slider for it.
screen.COMET_TAIL_VOLUME=Tail Volume
option.COMET_TAIL_STEPS=Volume Steps
option.COMET_TAIL_STEPS.comment=Samples taken along the tail. More is smoother but costs more. Too few gives concentric banding (a per-pixel jitter softens it).
option.COMET_TAIL_DENSITY=Density Falloff
option.COMET_TAIL_DENSITY.comment=How fast the tail fades along its length (applied to the squared axis coordinate).
option.COMET_TAIL_TAPER=Far-End Taper
option.COMET_TAIL_TAPER.comment=How fast the tail narrows toward its tip. Smaller tapers faster and gives a sharper point. At 0.80 the widest point sits around the middle, matching the reference photos.
option.COMET_TAIL_BEND_POW=Curvature Distribution
option.COMET_TAIL_BEND_POW.comment=How the bend is spread along the tail. 1.2 = it starts curving immediately; 2.0 = straight near the nucleus then a sharp hook. Only visible once the curvature sliders are raised.
option.COMET_TAIL_SHARP=Radial Sharpness
option.COMET_TAIL_SHARP.comment=Softness of the tail cross-section. 1.0 is a smooth flare (recommended). Above 2 it goes back to the hard-edged straight slab look.
option.COMET_TAIL_ANALYTIC=Fall Back to Analytic Shell
option.COMET_TAIL_ANALYTIC.comment=For A/B comparison only. Switches back to the old analytic shape (hard straight edges, the two slabs in the earlier screenshot) to confirm the volume version really is better.
value.COMET_TAIL_ANALYTIC.0=Volume marching (new)
value.COMET_TAIL_ANALYTIC.1=Analytic shell (old, hard edges)
